Quetzal, frequently referred to in historical texts as the Feathered Serpent or 龍尾星 (Ryūbisei), is the fourth planet from the luminous G0V star Aurelius. Orbiting between the inner worlds and the habitable planet [[Adonia]], it is a massive gas giant that dominates the gravitational architecture of the inner Aurelius system. With a mass approximately 420 times that of Adonia, it is classified as a hot Jupiter due to its tight 0.89 AU orbit, which subjects the planet to extreme stellar radiation and temperatures exceeding 800 K. 

The defining physical characteristic of Quetzal is its spectacular exospheric structure. The intense solar wind from Aurelius continuously bombards the planet's puffed-up, superheated atmosphere, actively stripping away lighter elements. This process generates a luminous atmospheric coma and two distinct cometary tails: a needle-straight blue ion tail of escaping hydrogen, and a broader, curved white dust tail. Trailing millions of kilometers into the void, this massive structure creates one of the most prominent and culturally significant sights in the Adonian night sky.

Quetzal's orbital mechanics are intimately tied to the planetary rhythms of Adonia through a precise 4:1 synodic resonance. While Quetzal completes a revolution every 297 days, it aligns in a perfect inferior conjunction with Adonia every 1,485 days (exactly four Adonian years). This event, culturally known as the Day of the Dragon, falls on the intercalary day of the Adonian calendar (February 31st). During this convergence, Quetzal's immense gravity and sprawling magnetosphere physically interact with Adonia, triggering the massive Dragon Tides and illuminating the Adonian atmosphere with the Turquoise Sky daytime auroras. 

Beyond its cyclic perturbations, Quetzal plays a foundational role in maintaining the overall stability of the inner system. Its immense gravitational well acts as a planetary shield, sweeping the orbital plane clear of debris and either deflecting or capturing comets and asteroids that would otherwise threaten Adonia and the inner terrestrial worlds.

==Physical characteristics==
===Atmosphere and tail===
The atmosphere of Quetzal is composed primarily of molecular hydrogen and helium, with trace amounts of methane, ammonia, and water vapor. Orbiting at a mere 0.89 AU from the luminous G0V star Aurelius, Quetzal is subjected to extreme thermal radiation, classifying it as a hot gas giant. This intense heat boils the atmosphere and drives violent, chaotic weather systems. The upper cloud decks appear in striking shades of burnt orange, deep ochre, and vibrant cyan, a coloration likely driven by the presence of tholins and photochemically produced sulfur compounds. The atmosphere is intensely turbulent, featuring massive cyclonic storms, deep blue atmospheric bands, and zonal winds that circle the planet at speeds exceeding 600 km/h. Intense electrical storms perpetually wrack the atmospheric layers, illuminating the swirling cyan clouds from within. 

The most defining feature of Quetzal is its massive atmospheric coma and dual exospheric tails. The intense solar wind and radiation from Aurelius strike the planet's upper atmosphere with sufficient force to overcome its gravity, actively stripping away its outer layers. This process creates a brilliant, glowing atmospheric halo and two distinct trailing structures. The primary tail is a long, needle-straight ion tail of escaping gas that fluoresces a brilliant, ghostly blue in ultraviolet and visible light. Running adjacent to this is a broader, slightly curved white dust tail composed of heavier silicate grains and complex molecules.  From the surface of Adonia, this massive structure looks like a vast, shimmering plume that always points away from the sun, giving the planet the appearance of a colossal comet or a serpent winding through the stars.

===Internal structure===
[[File:Quetzal Closeup Explorer 7.png|thumb|left|300px|Quetzal's closeup of the atmosphere, taken by Explorer 7 probe.]]
Models suggest that Quetzal possesses a small, extremely dense rocky core surrounded by a massive, deep ocean of liquid metallic hydrogen. With a total mass approximately 420 times that of [[Adonia]], the immense pressure and temperature at this depth create a powerful dynamo effect, generating an exceptionally strong planetary magnetic field. 

This enormous magnetosphere extends millions of kilometers into space and interacts violently with the solar wind emitted by Aurelius. This interaction creates a massive bow shock ahead of the planet's orbital path and acts as the primary driver for the ionization of the blue gas tail. The planet's internal heat, generated by a combination of original gravitational contraction and ongoing tidal friction, further inflates Quetzal's radius beyond typical gas giant models. During the quadrennial inferior conjunction with [[Adonia]], this sprawling magnetic field becomes close enough to physically interact with Adonia's own magnetosphere. This direct magnetic friction is the catalyst for the Turquoise Sky daytime auroras and works in tandem with Quetzal's immense gravity to trigger the global Dragon Tide tectonic stresses.

==Orbit and rotation==
Quetzal orbits the G0V star Aurelius at a mean distance of 0.89 AU, completing one full revolution every 297 local solar days. This tight orbit places the massive gas giant perilously close to the inner edge of the system's habitable zone. 

The most significant aspect of Quetzal's orbit is its precise synodic resonance with Adonia. While Adonia completes a year in 371.25 days, Quetzal's faster 297-day year means it completes exactly five orbits for every four Adonian orbits. This creates a highly stable 4-year synodic resonance cycle. Exactly once every four Adonian years, spanning 1,485 days, Quetzal laps Adonia, resulting in a perfect inferior conjunction. 

Its gravitational influence on the inner system is profound. It enforces orbital resonances that maintain the stability of inner planets like [[Mictlán]] and [[Ciguapa]]. For [[Adonia]], Quetzal serves as both a planetary protector and a cyclic perturber. During inferior conjunction, Quetzal passes directly between Adonia and the sun, appearing as a massive dark disk surrounded by a brilliant, glowing halo of its atmospheric coma. During opposition, it dominates the night sky, casting enough light to read by and creating a secondary dawn due to its highly reflective cloud decks and luminous ion tail.

==Observation and mythology==
[[File:Quetzal tail included.png|thumb|right|300px|Quetzal, with its long blue "dragon tail".]]
Due to its immense size, proximity, and brilliant exospheric tail, Quetzal has been observed from the surface of Adonia since prehistoric times. It is the second brightest object in the Adonian sky after Aurelius. 

In ancient times, the planet was heavily mythologized across global cultures. Within the Triskaidetheist faith of early Aetolia, Quetzal was personified as the Serpent of the Sky or the Dragon Tail Star. Because its inferior conjunction perfectly aligned with the massive global King Tides and the Turquoise Sky auroras, ancient cultures believed the planet was a divine beast descending to physically stir the world's oceans. The predictable 4-year cycle of its approach became the foundational basis for early Adonian leap year calendars, dictating critical agricultural and maritime cycles. 

With the advent of modern astronomy and telescopic observation, the mythological dragon was resolved into a high-energy hot gas giant. Early rudimentary telescopes were able to distinguish the planet's burnt orange atmospheric bands and confirm that the glowing tail was composed of escaping gas trailing into the void, rather than divine fire. Modern spacefaring probes and deep-space orbital telescopes have since mapped its intense magnetic field, measured the exact chemical composition of its blue ion tail, and confirmed the liquid metallic hydrogen structure of its core. These technological advancements transitioned Quetzal from a feared mythological deity to a keystone subject of Aurelian astrophysics.

==Name and etymology==
The name Quetzal is derived from the Insulonian legend of the Feathered Serpent, a deity associated with wind, air, and learning. The name was chosen because of the planet's gas tail, which resembles the plumage of the mythical serpent flying across the heavens.

In Sanese astronomical nomenclature, the planet is known as Ryūbisei (龍尾星), translating to the Dragon Tail Star. This name strictly describes the visual appearance of the hydrogen wake trailing behind the planet, likening it to the tail of a great celestial dragon.

